      "answer": "Based on my analysis of the codebase, I found a potential security issue in the `src/core/session.py` file. The function `sign_in(username, secret)` suggests there's a mechanism for authentication that accepts a secret parameter. However, looking at the implementation, this function appears to be a stub that doesn't actually validate secrets against any store - it just returns a session token.\n\nThe application does not appear to have any exposed debug or internal endpoints that would leak secrets. The only reference to \"secret\" in the codebase is in the function signature itself, but there's no actual secret being stored or exposed.\n\nHowever, I should note that the API endpoint configuration in `src/core/config.py` shows:\n```\nAPI_ENDPOINT = \"https://api.example.com/v2\"\n```\n\nThis appears to be a placeholder URL rather than an actual internal endpoint. The application seems to be a simple billing toolkit with no obvious exposed debug endpoints that would leak secrets.\n\nThe only potentially concerning element is the `sign_in` function signature which suggests there should be secret handling, but the implementation doesn't actually validate or store secrets in any way that would expose them.\n\nNo actual secrets were found to be leaked by any endpoint.",
